Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Chris,
I'm trying to convert some combined argos and fastloc (gps) data into a telemetry object. So I've followed some previous advice and split the data into 2 separate data set to convert each one then tbind them back together. But when I bring the data back together the COV.x.x and COV.y.y are off by a factor or 2. I know there some some issue about the root 2 factor the for the semi major/minor argos conversion, etc. I'm just not sure if I should fix this in the gps UERE or it's fine as is.

I can demo with the pelican data:

data(pelican)
uere(pelican) <- 10 #I'd like to fix this as I do for my data
ppp <- tbind(pelican)
ppp[1:6,]

if I look at row 4 (first gps) I would expect that COV.x.x (and COV.y.y) would be equal to (UERE * HDOP)^2 [ =(10*2)^2) ] but it is (UERE * HDOP)^2/2 same with the 6th row. In terms of the SSM I'd like to fix the error rates with the former. But I'm not sure if the tbind was just making them match the argos and that 2 will be accounted for in the ctmm functions, or should I adjust the UERE by a sqrt(2) factor. If this has been discussed before or there are descriptions of the telemetry object columns that will give the answer, just point me in that direction.

Thanks!

From u40fd627580dd34f5 at groups.google.com  Fri May 26 23:01:09 2023
From: u40fd627580dd34f5 at groups.google.com (Christen Fleming)
Date: Fri, 26 May 2023 23:01:09 +0000
Subject: Re: What is the correct way to aggregate individual ODs at the population level?
Message-ID: <W_Xo5jnHFEA.ODDHgQMEAgAJ@ctmm-user.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <W_Xo5jnHFEA.GY6poO_HAQAJ@ctmm-user.groups.google.com>
References: <W_Xo5jnHFEA.GY6poO_HAQAJ@ctmm-user.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ctmm-user/c/W_Xo5jnHFEA/m/ODDHgQMEAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Dani,

It depends on what you want the aggregate population OD to correspond to. The individual OD corresponds to asking where the animal is located after sampling a random time (uniformly) from the sampling period  For multiple individuals:
Sampling a random individual and then sampling a random time from that individual's sampling period would correspond to the unweighted mean.
Sampling a random time from the sampling period of all individuals and then sampling a random individual would correspond to the weighted mean, where the weights are proportional to the sampling period of each individual.
2. makes more sense to me. The difference in scaling should come from either the difference in sampling period or sampling frequency. If the frequencies are the same, then shorter tracks should just have more probability mass in a smaller area, and the weights should account for that.

Best,
Chris

