Hi Tori,

The x-axis on the variogram is relative time (time lags between data points), where as segmentation happens at absolute times. Sometimes you can see resident behaviors up to a certain timescale in the variogram, which would tell you how long a segment or window might be, but it can't tell you where to place the segment.

This variogram appears range resident, but with either a daily periodicity or duty cycling or something. If there is a day or two of initial dispersal, then it's not readily visible in the variogram. I would suggest plotting the locations colored by time to see that.

I think people mostly do this kind of segmentation by hand or work out some species-specific thresholds on statistics like daily displacement, etc..

I'm actually right in the middle of coding a segmentation fitting method that would estimate the most likely change point, assuming that there is one. I expect an early version to be out this summer.

Best,
Chris

